hift in the national environment, or escalation of the stock-trading story could tip the narrow edge either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Southwest Michigan's 4th District pairs a Republican-leaning electorate with a strong national Democratic climate, producing one of the closest House races of 2026. Five of nine raters call it a Tossup, three say Lean R, and one calls a Democratic flip. The only recent poll, a Democratic-sponsored survey from mid-September, put Sean McCann ahead of Bill Huizenga by a single point, 47 to 46.\n\nHuizenga, seeking another term, has out-raised McCann $4.1 million to $2.6 million and holds a cash edge, but outside groups have spent about $1.1 million attacking him while backing him with only about $0.5 million. Prediction markets give McCann roughly a 48 percent chance. A Detroit Metro Times report on Huizenga's stock disclosures has handed Democrats an attack line, and Giffords PAC is advertising for McCann. The seat remains a genuine coin flip with a slight Republican tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5309, "margin": 0.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4711, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4732, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4842, "margin": 0.3}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4178, "margin": -0.7}]}